#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

char freq[] = "ETAOINSHRDLCUMWFGYPBVKJXQZ";

int main() {
    char c[500], p[500], map[26];
    int count[26] = {0}, i, j, k, n = 10;

    printf("Enter ciphertext: ");
    fgets(c, sizeof(c), stdin);

    /* Count frequency of each letter */
    for(i = 0; c[i]; i++)
        if(isalpha(c[i]))
            count[toupper(c[i])-'A']++;

    /* Print possible plaintexts using frequency substitution */
    printf("\nTop %d possible plaintexts:\n", n);

    for(k = 0; k < n; k++) {
        int max = -1, pos = -1;

        /* Find most frequent unused ciphertext letter */
        for(i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if(count[i] > max) {
                max = count[i];
                pos = i;
            }
        }

        if(pos == -1 || max == 0) break;

        /* Map ciphertext frequency to English frequency */
        map[pos] = freq[k];

        /* Mark as used */
        count[pos] = -1;

        /* Display current possible plaintext */
        for(i = 0; c[i]; i++) {
            if(isalpha(c[i]))
                p[i] = map[toupper(c[i])-'A'];
            else
                p[i] = c[i];
        }
        p[i] = '\0';

        printf("%d. %s\n", k + 1, p);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Principle

English letters occur approximately in this order:

```text
E T A O I N S H R D L C U M W F G Y P B V K J X Q Z
```

The program:

1. Counts the frequency of letters in the ciphertext.
2. Finds the most frequent ciphertext letters.
3. Maps them to common English letters.
4. Produces possible plaintexts.
5. Displays up to **10 candidates**.

**Note:** A true automatic attack on a general monoalphabetic substitution cipher normally uses **word/dictionary scoring, n-gram frequencies, or hill-climbing**, because simple letter frequency alone cannot reliably recover the exact plaintext. This version is intentionally short for an exam.
